          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Chris:</strong> Coding standards are critical, as when a developer writes code, they need to make sure that any other developer in the same team can understand it. A developer who is not consistent with their coding items such as naming conventions, will run into problems later down the line, especially if they are working as part of a team. If each developer in a team is writing code in their own style, then this will certainly cause issues.</p>
<p>The consequences of not following coding standards can compromise security and code performance issues and would make it difficult to maintain or update the code.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Security concerns</p>
<p>If code is inconsistent, contains bugs or has logical errors, then the software will be more vulnerable to security attacks. This is especially the case with complex code [e.g. not DRY], and errors in the code.</p>
<p>Maintaining ‘Coding standards’ can reduce code complexity, the number of bugs, and improve the debugging process as they would help the team to prevent errors or detect issues earlier.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Performance issues</p>
<p>Coding standards help reduce the performance issues of the software that may arise due to the result of poor coding practices. So, for example if a program was complex, it could take longer to execute or perform in the way it was expected. This would not only have an impact on its performance, but also on the client or customer or the end user’s confidence and expectations.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Difficulty in maintaining or updating the code</p>
<p>If there are no standards and each developer is following their own style, then this makes it exceedingly difficult and time-consuming for other developers within the team to interpret the code.</p>
<p>It is crucial for a coding team to have proper standards to maintain quality, so any developer in the team can understand any part of the code that another developer has written and change or update it independently. It can help new developers joining the team giving them clear guidelines and enabling them to fit in more easily. It would reduce the development cost and time as the code could be reused and create better team integration as the code would be consistent.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
